## What this session did

**Landed part 2 of the step-4a mount-ordering fix — build 0.11.401, srcversion `EF59ED0039F7ED8EF3B03D0`, compiles clean. It must NOT be boarded.**

The code change is complete as designed:

- `mxfs_dlm_invalidate_cached_views(mp)` split out of `mxfs_dlm_peer_joined_flush` — cached-state drops only, no log force / AIL push, so it is legal before `xfs_log_mount_finish`.
- New `mxfs_dlm_mount_recovery_barrier(mp)`: flush → confirm/fence/mark-pending the step-6.5 cohort → inline foreign-slice replay per slot → cohort publish → own-slot reclaim.
- Called from `xfs_mountfs()` right after `xfs_log_mount()` (`xfs/xfs_mount.c`, + the missing `xfs_mxfs_dlm.h` include), ~105 lines ahead of `xfs_log_mount_finish` — the first cluster-lock taker.
- `mxfs_dlm_mount_recovery_settle` trimmed to the two things needing a live mount.
- `TIMEOUT_BUDGETS.md` records the 62 s (31 × 2000 ms) conditional mount cost and exactly who pays it.

**Then the RULE-5 consult ruled STOP-SHIP, and it was right.** The most serious finding is in code I wrote this session: fence-failure residue is left for the *post*-`xfs_mountfs` retry, but `xfs_log_mount_finish` runs *inside* `xfs_mountfs` and can block on an AG lock held by one of those unfenced slots — mount never returns, retry never runs. **That is the original bootstrap deadlock, restored by the fix for it.** No rig cycle was spent on a build that would have hung.

Seven further items, all recorded with specifics in ccmemory as `ccloop-c7ee71c6-sess57-barrier-landed-GPT-STOPSHIP-8-items`: publication (zeroing the dead HB slot) happens before recovered intents/iunlink/AGI-sweep complete; the foreign shadow AIL has no documented completion path; purge-then-reacquire of our own retained bits is not atomic against a peer; the invalidator declares an AG uncached even when it skipped buffers; completion needs a per-slot "safe to publish" mask rather than "anything replayed"; the publish sequence has unchecked irreversible steps; and cross-slice LSN comparability needs proving — that last one cross-checks an existing memory that already flagged "non-comparable per-node LSNs", so it may be a separate open defect.

I also found, reading `mxfs_blkdev_flush_epoch` for GPT's durability question, that it **skips `blkdev_issue_flush` entirely when `mxfs_fua_disable` is set** — the default since sess94. The barrier's step (a) therefore only bumps an epoch counter in the shipped config. The function's own rationale is a coherency argument, not a durability one; it is flagged for audit, not yet a proven defect.

I applied one of the eight myself (per-slot replayed mask instead of the whole cohort) since it was in-flight; the other seven are the next session's work, 6A first.

**Criteria: NO.** Not production ready — 11 open defects, 4 critical, and defect #1's mount-ordering fix is now fully written but carries a known deadlock that GPT identified and I have not yet fixed.

